  "id": "COMPOUND:7960 - PROTEIN:792",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Canrenoic acid may interact with Neuronal acetylcholine receptor subunit alpha-9 through a series of target relationships. Specifically, Canrenoic acid is linked to Neuronal acetylcholine receptor subunit beta-3, which in turn is targeted by varenicline, and varenicline further targets Neuronal acetylcholine receptor subunit alpha-9. Additionally, Canrenoic acid is also associated with Neuronal acetylcholine receptor subunit alpha-3, which is targeted by Patchouli alcohol, and Patchouli alcohol targets the same subunit alpha-9. These pathways indicate that Canrenoic acid may influence the function of the neuronal acetylcholine receptor subunit alpha-9 through different intermediaries, potentially via modulatory or inhibitory mechanisms. The evidence is consistent with a role for Canrenoic acid in regulating the activity of the neuronal acetylcholine receptor subunit alpha-9, though the exact mechanism of interaction remains to be elucidated. The strength of the evidence is moderate, with varying scores across the different paths, suggesting that the relationship is supported but not definitively established.",
